| # | guard | result |
|---|---|---|
| F1 | the direction 'toward the viewer' is read off the rig's own version; pinning it to 0.x's -Z dots the 1.0 body's palm against her back | RED |
| F2 | each family's rigSha is checked against ITS OWN body, so a clearance measured on another rig cannot pass as this one's | RED |
| F3 | the handInHead budget is held to what THIS body measures: raise it past the 0.212 read here and the guard reddens | RED |
| F4 | a clip whose ends stand 157mm off centre on this body needs this body to say so | RED |
| F5 | this family's pans are held to what clearance.panFor derives from ITS crowns (0.07 was the first pass, before the pan moved the projection it was solved against) | RED |
| F6 | a variant whose family is not in the registry is caught; this is what stops the describe.each above from quietly losing a pass | RED |
| F9 | the ?mika= and localStorage gate reads the OFFERED half, so a declared-but-unoffered body cannot be asked for by hand | RED |
| F10 | each family's generated reach is re-measured against its producer, so a .gen.ts that drifts from the code that writes it is caught | RED |
| F7 | screenX's mirror decides which edge is which, and the frame guard compares both against the sides the producer recorded | RED |
| F11 | a pan naming a clip outside the pool is refused, the way a waiver and a crownSeen entry already were | RED |
| F8 | a formatting-only edit must NOT redden the pan guard | GREEN |

